<article class="env-lemma" id="05V2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05V2">Lemma <span data-tag="05V2">7.34.3</span>.</a> Let $f : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{D}) \to \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ be a morphism of topoi. Let $q : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (pt) \to \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{D})$ be a point. Then $p = f \circ q$ is a point of the topos $\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ and we have a canonical identification </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  (f^{-1}\mathcal{F})_ q = \mathcal{F}_ p  \]
</div>
  <p> for any sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathcal{C}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is immediate from the definitions and the fact that we can compose morphisms of topoi. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>